> Primarily, heavily random with a fast-moving food clock (Rogue) as
> compared to nominally random with no clock at all (Nethack).

I don't think Nethack should adapt Rogue's semi-hard turn limit, but
when food became much less of an issue to the game, it subtly changed
the nature of the game.  Sometimes I wonder why Nethack has hunger at
all, except as a source of intrinsics and an extra thing to kill
inexperienced players.  Crawl has a better hunger system than
Nethack's, strict enough to prevent players from spending many tens of
thousands of turns doing nothing while loose enough that players
generally working towards winning the game, even if they take their
time at it, aren't restricted too much by it.

> If nobody will play it, the best design in the world is a failure.  While
